#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "e16376f2-ffb9-580c-a7e7-57f5814f8289" :question/math-academy-id "e-21137" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/c7/c7bdf20729aac1938756be1b2fa530004a77924e0c00b4d7f0fb8ddae7368ff1.svg)\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n![](images/d5/d52cacaec3d2c2e516c3b3cfa085e113bb39cd4dc1e6bcc50583ceae566f9c07.svg)\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n![](images/7c/7c9f6a4acce39bd7847045fb6faf2868aca76b9b85904dcbe4991202a18be7a9.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=7 \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c_1= 5 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_1 = 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n1 - c_2 = -1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_2 = 2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128953 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129313 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "d2768264-6d1a-5e62-9af4-025c877e138b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5608" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n![](images/8f/8fdedaad6e0d10a2bfab94f4266830a5efefbe2997d05455fecb35cbf7cefb43.svg)\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=-3.$*" :question/worked-solution "Let's construct the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system\n![](images/7a/7a07096a35f46528c18bdce5e711a5e813ac1923342e06cbff3f3c6b3215ae64.svg)\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= 2\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2= -3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =2,$ we get \n![](images/28/284acfc8cb4726bec4f4e805a8be0c037b38a0b9af287a27a4c7f5a2f1679d60.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/b3/b33588ca4b487e1de13aac19bdfc5347751dbe95a9f93c27729d7da54ab02e06.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =-3,$ we get \n![](images/1c/1c6b0e7303099895b50f884468ba6bfb7830ec88bbaea17dbc322a5a70eeea4c.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/bb/bb4d152439d5a97dfda5196a24752e2048fb421a8cfac5c67ffbab512115d0c8.svg)\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution and use the initial values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $c_2 = -1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-1=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =6[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128954 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "226a5f8f-ac26-5f3e-8f8f-f145c314820f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5609" :question/problem "Let $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=-2$ be eigenvalues of the $2\\times 2$ matrix  $A$ that correspond to eigenvectors \n![](images/be/bea45856123519703211eba01e1c6fe6b3cf8cd045eb7b2ad2a2a3a7cf9adc43.svg) \nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and that $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n![](images/62/62ced02d9670f7b749e19ea357508aeb57f6b65777d6c2dd5adc4d2e35172688.svg)\nfind the position of the particle after $2$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-11 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n16c_1 = -32\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_1=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2\\cdot(-2) -3c_2 = 1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_2=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $\\mathbf x(2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\cdot 2}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12{e}^{6}+{e}^{-4} \\\\ 4{e}^{6}-3{e}^{-4}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031fcf4c-44d6-5f96-acb1-353d67b1fad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389610} {:answer/id #uuid "8a74ffc8-d62f-510a-82cc-c909e16d7fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389611} {:answer/id #uuid "8513d342-7c71-558c-bb5b-2d0b8669c8c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389612} {:answer/id #uuid "89d6b4d2-63a0-58db-b23d-fe914857704c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389613} {:answer/id #uuid "ede6c55e-98c0-5183-847e-807c623a05f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8513d342-7c71-558c-bb5b-2d0b8669c8c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389612} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a4ed42-4ec3-5cae-83cc-1a4e796d7a8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d561dbfb-7ee3-58dc-8154-27aa2bc8812a" :question/math-academy-id "q-168916" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2(0)} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n6{c}_{1}=12\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2+2{c}_{2}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+1[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fde92cf-da54-5afe-9f1a-b3d21d6bf290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389617} {:answer/id #uuid "87090451-e0b4-56e6-a0c2-708d60d4f12e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4}+3{e}^{6} \\\\ -{e}^{-4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389618} {:answer/id #uuid "306f0ec4-5a23-5893-a77a-0c4ccd9d70a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4} \\\\ 5{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389619} {:answer/id #uuid "51a41736-479c-5cbe-a6a5-f920a289f460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ {e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389620} {:answer/id #uuid "8e43a50c-201c-59de-8f0a-2df24646008e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{6} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4}+{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fde92cf-da54-5afe-9f1a-b3d21d6bf290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389617} :answer-field/id #uuid "207eb684-605f-5bc6-8756-8f315ee86796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "048c4d19-f776-56af-aac6-30cdbc559443" :question/math-academy-id "q-168917" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=2$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(0)}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3(0)} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=3 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n4{c}_{1}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-1+{c}_{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(2)}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3(2)} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0490d2f0-fe28-5967-9617-bbb17cd67f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0490d2f0-fe28-5967-9617-bbb17cd67f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413745} :answer-field/id #uuid "4265eaf3-4298-5be3-9a5f-defa76c03f8b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413772} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e2b5b83-31df-56b6-880c-ad3705a2ea13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e2b5b83-31df-56b6-880c-ad3705a2ea13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413753} :answer-field/id #uuid "78faa26c-94b2-5ee2-86ab-c3fcb3b05b17" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413777} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82fec8d2-3786-50ca-a921-4689b6afec2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82fec8d2-3786-50ca-a921-4689b6afec2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413757} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0e110a-54da-543d-aa0a-cb20cc674499" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5041c10-08ae-56e7-bc0e-86c30d564ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5041c10-08ae-56e7-bc0e-86c30d564ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413765} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7da8384-bec0-52ca-95f9-6b880e18d11d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0eaa03d-11bc-5f47-92e3-86afc2afb668" :question/math-academy-id "q-321994" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ -{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=3 \\\\ -{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n4{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129313 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006c6611-e018-504a-a01c-32aef354674d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299933} {:answer/id #uuid "4867cad9-c101-5cc1-9ce1-f22a654e0225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300328} {:answer/id #uuid "89b4be77-7a0c-52fe-bdfe-13c390e402fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300658} {:answer/id #uuid "bead334f-835b-513e-937d-1e09bd4a9b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300913} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7d33b5-0d60-5b9d-afdb-fc4a819ff4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bead334f-835b-513e-937d-1e09bd4a9b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300913} :answer-field/id #uuid "1132d972-9068-5f5b-bea7-ae90f79c57d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2f46aeb-4fa8-5261-bc96-7f74682a5de2" :question/math-academy-id "q-324462" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4(0)}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-0} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 5{c}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=2.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=1.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129313 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab7e0ce9-0b69-5dd3-a368-3cc1da284569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370044} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e34118-c9d0-5956-a8ce-2eaaecfc99ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370045} {:answer/id #uuid "a42a97c5-ccd6-5b4d-9a83-19aeeb71c358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370046} {:answer/id #uuid "d56b7809-8b90-5f3f-871c-86a6b90b4486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370047} {:answer/id #uuid "a923eb6b-6333-5907-be70-a7184211d7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=5[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab7e0ce9-0b69-5dd3-a368-3cc1da284569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370044} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a329d6-e958-5db1-9f6a-f6e439f8337a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49d0e43c-ac04-5d4e-a46f-7b5d2747eccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-324500" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=9 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}=15\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=5$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+{c}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c6a383d-9e67-5d62-bd5b-52639d0e77ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c6a383d-9e67-5d62-bd5b-52639d0e77ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/re