ned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-5{c}_{1}=-5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3\\cdot 1+{c}_{2}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102c058e-dc31-5c85-9478-992372c3c8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value 